<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Lopez</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape Lopez is a headland at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogooue">Ogooué</a> delta on the coast of Gabon, near modern Port-Gentil, named after the 15th-century Portuguese navigator Lopo Gonçalves. Its sheltered bay made it a trading point for European ships. The Orungu kingdom controlled the trade there, first in enslaved people and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and other produce.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-lopez.jpg" alt="Cape Lopez" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_gabon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
